coder › Implemented `deepMerge(base, over)` on branch `work/deep-merge` in two small commits.

What changed:
- `src/merge.ts` (`Implement prototype-safe deepMerge`): deep-merges own-enumerable keys. Both sides plain objects (proto is `Object.prototype` or `null`) → recurse; otherwise `over` value deep-cloned and replaces. `undefined` in `over` is skipped, `null` replaces. Own keys `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype` in `over` are skipped at every level. Nothing is mutated: `base` is deep-cloned first, `over` values are deep-cloned on assignment, so the result shares no object with either input. Plain-object check means class instances/Dates/arrays are replaced, not merged. Internal writes go through a `setOwn` helper that uses `defineProperty` for a literal `__proto__` key so cloning a `base` that happens to own that key can't rewire a prototype.
- `src/merge.test.ts` (`Add deepMerge tests incl. __proto__ pollution`): kept the existing flat test; added recursive merge, array-replace, scalar/object replace both ways, `undefined`-skips vs `null`-replaces, unsafe-keys ignored, dedicated `JSON.parse('{"__proto__":...}')` pollution test, no-mutation, no-shared-reference tests.

Checks (project has only `bun test`):
- `bun test` → `9 pass, 0 fail, 22 expect() calls, Ran 9 tests across 1 file.`
- Extra typecheck (no tsconfig in repo): `bunx --package typescript tsc --noEmit --strict --target es2022 --module nodenext src/merge.ts` → exit 0. Checking the test file too reports only pre-existing `bun:test` module-resolution and `import './merge.ts'` extension noise plus intentional `polluted`-on-`{}` assertions, so I verified the type error fix for the latter and left the harness-specific ones alone.

Decisions not in the brief: `constructor`/`prototype` filtering applies recursively (not just top level), and “plain object” is proto-equals-`Object.prototype`-or-`null` (so Dates, Maps, class instances replace rather than merge).

